      "marks": 6,
      "answer": "$z=-3-i$ or $z=-1-3i$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator: $\\frac{(x+4)+iy}{x+i(y+4)}\\times\\frac{x-i(y+4)}{x-i(y+4)}$. *M1. Equate the imaginary part of the numerator to zero. DM1; numerator $=x(x+4)+y(y+4)+i[xy-(y+4)(x+4)]$. Obtain $x+y=-4$. A1 OE. Use the modulus correctly and solve for $x$ or $y$. DM1; $x^2+(-x-4)^2=10\\Rightarrow x^2+4x+3=0$, or $(-y-4)^2+y^2=10\\Rightarrow y^2+4y+3=0$. Obtain $z=-3-i$. A1; one correct solution, with SC A1 A0 for both pairs of $x,y$ correct but not in the given form. Obtain $z=-1-3i$. A1; two solutions only. Alternative method 1: set $z+4=c(z+4i)$, $c\\in\\mathbb{R}$, or $x+iy+4=c(x+iy+4i)$. *M1. Equate real and imaginary parts: $x+4=cx$, $y=cy+4c$. DM1. Obtain $x=\\frac4{c-1}$ and $y=\\frac{4c}{1-c}$, or $\\frac{y}{x+4}=\\frac{y+4}{x}$. A1. Use the modulus correctly and solve for $c$, $x$ or $y$. DM1; $6c^2+20c+6=0$ OE, or either equivalent quadratic above. $c=-\\frac13$ leads to $z=-3-i$. A1, with the same SC guidance. $c=-3$ leads to $z=-1-3i$. A1; two solutions only. Alternative method 2: use $\\arg(z+4)=\\arg(z+4i)$, equivalently $\\arg\\left(\\frac{z+4}{z+4i}\\right)=0$. *M1. Obtain $\\frac{y}{x+4}=\\frac{y+4}{x}$. DM1. Obtain $x+y=-4$. A1. Continue with the same modulus work and the same two final solutions and guidance. DM1 A1 A1. Total: [6]",
